tasks.task:
The owner approved the Game SDK design in chat on 27 Nov 2026. I asked: "Approve the core and take the recommended scope answers?" (after reporting dogfood round 4: all four games would lock in the core, confidence 7-8/10). The owner said: "I approve the sdk plan."

Record that approval in the design's Decisions, and answer the scope questions in "Scope questions for the owner" with the planner's recommendations, as approved: 1 yes (3D collision queries and a kinematic move, no rigid bodies); 2 yes (gamepads); 3 yes (audio, small form); 4 no, the release after (predicted projectiles and contact); 5 no, after 4 (hits as the shooter saw them); 6 already yes (3D multiplayer); 7 yes (headless host); 8 skeletal animation stays out, first engine module after; 9 no (text field, lists); 10 after the first release (editor levels); 11 yes (per-player profiles). Add the work packages and engine modules those yeses need.

Then fold in the four core frictions from dogfood round 4 (reports in tmp/sdk-dogfood-4/*/REPORT.md), each as an addition to the core, not a reshaping:
- saves: a save field that captures and respawns the entities of named prefabs/components by stable prefab name, so a save is not a hand-written mirror of the world (explorer-3d);
- ownership: a spawn that brings its children with a "spawned" moment, and a query that reads a parent's children (swarm-2d-online);
- PerPlayer departure: a "player left" rule can read the departing player's final per-player values for that tick (arena-3d-online);
- sweeps: a sweep result exposes the moving body's position and remaining motion at first contact (platformer-2d).
Read the other round-4 friction and extension notes and take any that are additions of the same kind. Keep the core's shape and names. Leave WP1 ready to build.
